## Phase 2: Stage 2 Core (Deep Learning)
Implement and validate the core deep learning detector (TransECA-Net).

5.  **Stage 2 Training (TransECA-Net) [Done]**
    *   **Objective**: Train the second-stage model to process Stage 1's "Suspicious" traffic.
    *   **Data**: `data/stage2/{train,val,test}.parquet` — 235,556/33,643/67,317 samples, 76 features, 15 classes.
    *   **Configuration**: d_model=128, nhead=8, num_layers=3, BS=512, 30 epochs, AdamW + CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts, XPU (Intel ARC 130T).
    *   **Results**: Test Acc 93.00%, Best Val Acc 92.52%, Weighted F1 0.95. Training time 64.18 min.
    *   **Outputs**: `models_chk/stage2_transeca.pth`, `results/stage1_report.txt`.

6.  **E8 (Ablation Study) [Done]**
    *   **Objective**: Demonstrate the effectiveness of the TransECA-Net architecture.
    *   **Comparison**: Full TransECA-Net vs. No-ECA (CNN+Trans) vs. CNN-Only, 20 epochs each.
    *   **Results**:
        | Variant | Params | Test Acc | W-F1 | M-F1 |
        |---|---|---|---|---|
        | Full TransECA-Net | 301,460 | 89.71% | 0.9248 | 0.7593 |
        | No-ECA (CNN+Trans) | 301,455 | 92.05% | 0.9470 | 0.6748 |
        | CNN-Only | 2,703 | 61.89% | 0.6677 | 0.2350 |
    *   **Component Contribution**: Transformer +0.2793 W-F1 (core component), ECA -0.0222 W-F1 (but M-F1 +0.0845, improving minority class recognition).
    *   **Conclusion**: Transformer encoder is the decisive architectural component; ECA slightly reduces weighted metrics but improves macro-average F1 for minority attack classes.
    *   **Outputs**: `results/E8_ablation_results.json`, `results/E8_ablation_report.txt`, `results/E8_ablation_comparison.png`, `results/E8_ablation_bar.png`.
    *   **Training Time**: 446.5 min (including sleep).

7.  **E15 (Generalization) [Done]**
    *   **Objective**: Validate TransECA-Net architecture generalization on UNSW-NB15.
    *   **Method**: Approach B — Architecture Generalization, training TransECA-Net from scratch on UNSW-NB15.
    *   **Data**: `archive/UNSW_NB15_{training,testing}-set.parquet` — Train 149,039 / Val 26,302 / Test 82,332, 186 features (One-hot), 10 classes.
    *   **Configuration**: d_model=128, nhead=8, num_layers=3, BS=512, 30 epochs, AdamW + CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts, XPU.
    *   **Results**:
        | Dataset | Test Acc | W-F1 | M-F1 |
        |---|---|---|---|
        | CIC-IDS2017 (S2) | 93.00% | 0.9500 | 0.80 |
        | UNSW-NB15 (E15) | 63.64% | 0.7034 | 0.3966 |
    *   **Per-class Highlights**: Generic F1 0.97, Reconnaissance F1 0.80, Normal Precision 0.99 but Recall 0.57, small classes (Analysis/Worms/Shellcode) remain challenging.
    *   **Conclusion**: The same architecture can be applied to different IDS datasets without modification; W-F1 of 0.70 demonstrates cross-dataset generalization capability.
    *   **Outputs**: `results/E15_generalization_report.txt`, `results/E15_generalization_results.json`, `results/E15_unsw_training_curves.png`, `results/E15_unsw_confusion_matrix.png`, `results/E15_cross_dataset_comparison.png`, `models_chk/e15_transeca_unsw.pth`.
    *   **Training Time**: 150.3 min.

